## PART C — Close the two bugs already known from the last integration pass

### Task 5 — `record_usage()` is never called on agent-mode turns

In `chat_handler`'s `if use_agent:` branch, no `record_usage()` call exists — it only happens in the `else` (direct mode) branch. Every agentic turn is invisible to `/status`, the admin dashboard, and daily/rate-limit-adjacent stats, despite costing real OpenRouter tokens (often more than direct mode, since it can run multiple LLM calls per turn). Add a `record_usage()` call at the end of the agent branch. Since `agent_meta` doesn't carry a clean token count, either sum a rough estimate across iterations (`len(response)//4` per LLM call, tracked in `AgentState`) or record with `tokens=0` and `msg_type="agent"` so at least message counts and rate limiting stay accurate — pick whichever the user prefers, but don't leave it uncounted.

### Task 6 — `should_use_agent()` triggers are too loose

`AGENT_TRIGGERS` includes bare words like `"code"`, `"plan"`, `"compare"`, `"analyze"`, `"the current"`, `"what's the"` — these will catch a lot of plain conversation that needs no tool ("what's the deal with...", "help me plan my week", "compare these two ideas"), pushing it through the slower, more expensive multi-call agent loop for nothing. Tighten the trigger list to require tool-shaped phrasing (verb + object pairs like "weather in", "calculate", a detected URL, a detected math expression) rather than single loose keywords. Add a log line recording `should_use_agent()`'s decision vs. whether a tool actually got called on that turn, so you can see false-positive rate in production and keep tuning it with real data instead of guessing.

---

## PART D — Architecture gaps: what OpenClaw/Hermes-style agents have that this doesn't yet

These are bigger, real gaps between what's built and what the architecture your own README describes actually implies at full maturity. Do them in this order — each builds on the last.

### Task 7 — Replace text-parsed tool calls with native function-calling

**Problem:** Tool calls currently work by asking the model to emit a `[TOOL_CALL]{"tool": ..., "args": {...}}[/TOOL_CALL]` block in free text, which `parse_tool_call()` then regexes out. This is fragile — a model can format it slightly wrong, wrap it in markdown fences, or emit prose before/after that confuses the parser — and it's not how OpenRouter/most modern model APIs are meant to be used for tool calling.

**Fix:** Switch to OpenRouter's native `tools` parameter (OpenAI-compatible function-calling schema) — most models routed through OpenRouter support it. Convert each `Skill`'s frontmatter (name, description, arguments) into a JSON-schema `tools` entry at request time in `context.build_context()` / `agent.py`'s request payload, and read `response.choices[0].message.tool_calls` instead of regexing the text. This removes an entire class of parsing failures and is the single highest-leverage change in this list. Keep the `[TOOL_CALL]` text format as a fallback parse path for models that don't support native tool calling, but make native calling the default.

**Verify:** Run the same multi-tool trace from the previous pass and confirm tool calls now arrive via `message.tool_calls` rather than being regexed from `message.content`, and that a deliberately malformed model response (ask a weaker/free model to reduce reliability) fails more gracefully than before.

### Task 8 — Upgrade episodic memory from keyword overlap to semantic search

**Problem:** `search_episodic()` in `utils/memory.py` does pure keyword-set overlap (`query_words & episode_words`) against the last 50 Firestore docs. This misses paraphrased or semantically related past interactions — a genuinely Hermes-style recall system needs embedding-based similarity, not literal word overlap.

**Fix:** At `store_episode()` time, generate an embedding of `query + summary` (OpenRouter/OpenAI-compatible embeddings endpoint, or any embedding model available through your existing OpenRouter key) and store the vector alongside the Firestore doc. At `search_episodic()` time, embed the incoming query and rank stored episodes by cosine similarity instead of word-set overlap. Firestore doesn't do native vector search at small scale without extensions, so for the current data volume (fetch-50-and-filter is already the existing pattern) it's fine to keep fetching a batch and ranking in Python — just rank by embedding similarity instead of word overlap. Note the added latency/cost per stored episode and per search (one embedding call each) and decide with the user whether that trade-off is worth it now vs. deferred.

**Verify:** Store two episodes that are semantically related but share few literal words (e.g. "how do I convert celsius to fahrenheit" and "temperature unit conversion help") and confirm the second surfaces the first in `search_episodic_memory()` results — the current keyword approach would miss this.

### Task 9 — Multi-agent orchestration (explicitly deferred in your own README)

**Problem:** The agent loop is single-agent by design right now (`README.md` line 50: "single agent (multi-agent is future)"). A fuller OpenClaw-style gateway supports delegating sub-tasks to specialized sub-agents (e.g. a research sub-agent that itself loops through several tool calls and returns a synthesized result to the orchestrating agent) rather than one flat loop handling everything.

**Fix (scope this as its own follow-up, don't rush it into this pass):** Add a `sub_agent` skill type — a skill whose `run()` doesn't call an external API but instead spins up a new `Agent.run()` instance with a narrower system prompt and its own tool subset, returning a synthesized text result to the parent loop like any other tool result. Cap sub-agent depth at 1 level (no sub-agents spawning sub-agents) to keep iteration/time budgets sane under Vercel's 60s limit. This is the biggest single feature gap in the list — treat it as a distinct project phase after Tasks 1–8 are stable, not something to bolt on quickly.

### Task 10 — No tests exist anywhere in the repo

**Problem:** There is no test file anywhere in the codebase. Every verification so far (in this pass and the last) has been manual live-message tracing, which doesn't scale and won't catch regressions as this grows (especially once Task 7's function-calling migration and Task 9's sub-agents land).

**Fix:** Add a `tests/` directory with, at minimum: unit tests for `parse_tool_call()` / the new native-tool-call parsing from Task 7, `find_skills_for_message()` trigger matching, `should_use_agent()`'s routing decisions (a table of sample messages → expected True/False, updated as Task 6 tunes it), and `reflect_on_task()`'s JSON-parsing/skill-creation logic with a mocked `send_message()`. Full end-to-end tests against live Telegram/OpenRouter/Firestore aren't practical to automate cheaply — keep those as the manual trace checklist from the previous pass, but everything that's pure logic (parsing, routing, formatting) should have real unit coverage.

### Task 11 — No observability across agent iterations

**Problem:** Logging exists (`logger.info`/`logger.error` throughout) but there's no way to trace a single user request across its full lifecycle — which iterations happened, which skills fired, what the reflector decided, all correlated to one request — without manually grepping timestamps. As Task 9 adds sub-agents this gets much harder to debug without structure.

**Fix:** Generate a short request ID (e.g. `chat_id + timestamp` hash) at the top of `chat_handler`, thread it through `Agent.run()`, `AgentState`, and the reflector calls, and include it in every log line for that request (`logger.info(f"[{request_id}] ...")`). This is cheap to add now and expensive to retrofit later once sub-agents exist.

---

## Do-last checklist

After all of the above, run the full trace one more time end to end:
1. Cold start → plain message → SOUL tone present, no agent path, usage recorded.
2. Single-tool message → one skill fires via native function-calling (Task 7), usage recorded (Task 5), no reflection.
3. Multi-tool message → reflection fires, learned skill created, `consolidate_memory` writes, episode stored with an embedding (Task 8).
4. Repeat a semantically similar (not identical) request → confirm the learned skill *and* the semantically related episode both surface.
5. `/forgetskill` removes the learned skill cleanly (Task 2).
6. Deliberately try the code_runner sandbox-escape pattern from Task 1 and confirm it's actually blocked.
7. Fresh `vercel --prod` deploy from a clean environment using the corrected `vercel.json` (Task 3) — confirm Firestore connects on first real message, not just in local dev.

That's the full remaining distance from "correctly integrated" (where the last pass left it) to "actually matches what a complete OpenClaw-gateway + Hermes-learning-loop system implies" — security-hardened, fully on Firebase with no stale trail, native tool-calling, semantic memory, and enough test/observability scaffolding that the next feature (multi-agent) can be built without flying blind.
